;; Docs for text/bjodat.bjo, read through (:doc #:file "bjodat.bjodoc"). The
;; texts are samizdat markup: code in prose is @code{...}. The names this
;; module re-exports from (text bjodat-core) are documented there.

(:doc #:module
  (summary "Records that read and write themselves as bjodat: declare one, get its decoder and encoder.")
  (see def/bjodat-type ->bjodat bjodat->)
  (example """
    (import (text bjodat))

    (def/bjodat-type
      (: Dep (Record (: name string)
                     (: version string #:default "*")
                     (: note (Option string) #:optional))))

    (bjodat-parse-Dep "{:name \"http\" :version \"1.2\"}")  ; (Ok Dep)
    (bjodat->string (Dep->bjodat d))                        ; "{:name \"http\" ...}"
    """)
  (reference """
    bjodat is Bjolang's shapes read as @em{data}, and is described with
    @code{(text bjodat-core)}, which holds the value, the reader and the
    writer. This module declares records that are read from it and written
    to it. @code{bjo/manifest.bjo} is a worked example of everything here:
    tagged forms, @code{#:rest}, a union dispatched on its head symbol, and
    hand-written instances for types the generator never heard of. It is an
    ordinary user of this module and gets no special case anywhere in it.

    @code{bjodat-parse-<Name>} reads the text @strong{straight into the
    record}. There is no intermediate tree: the key is compared against the
    field names in the reader's own buffer, so a record costs no key string,
    no interned @code{Keyword}, no node and no map. That is the interface to
    reach for.

    @strong{The two modules.}

    @read-table{
    | @code{(text bjodat)}      | @code{def/bjodat-type}, the traits, and what they generate |
    | @code{(text bjodat-core)} | the @code{Bjodat} value, the reader and the writer         |
    }

    @code{(text bjodat)} is enough for everything but the tree: the
    generator, the traits, @code{bjodat->string}, and hand-written instances
    naming @code{BjoInt} and the other cases. The code
    @code{def/bjodat-type} writes is spliced into @em{your} module and names
    @code{Bjodat} and @code{Reader} in its signatures, and this module
    re-exports both: one spelling of @code{bjodat-core}'s declarations, not a
    copy of them, so a @code{Bjodat} built either side of the boundary is one
    type. Re-exporting a union re-exports its cases.

    Import @code{(text bjodat-core)} as well when you want the @em{tree}:
    @code{parse-bjodat}, @code{read-bjodat}, @code{bjodat-ref} and the entry
    accessors, for data whose shape is not known until run time.

    These were once always imported together, on the belief that a type
    could not cross a facade. What cannot cross is a type @em{declared}
    twice; what a re-export publishes is the declaring module's declaration
    under a spelling of the facade's. See @code{re-export} in
    @code{Docs/MODULES.org}.

    @strong{Instances for a type of your own.} The generated decoders reach a
    field's type through its @code{bjodat->} instance, so a type the macro
    never heard of works as soon as it has one:

    @codeblock[#:lang "bjolang"]|{
    (type (: Port (Record (: number int))))

    (impl (->bjodat Port)
      (defun (->bjodat p) (BjoInt (cast System.Int64 (record-ref p number)))))

    (impl (bjodat-> Port)
      (defun (bjodat-> v)
        (match v
          ((BjoInt n) (Ok (Port (number (cast System.Int32 n)))))
          (_ (Err "expected a port number")))))

    (def/bjodat-type (: Server (Record (: host string) (: port Port))))
    }|

    @strong{Errors.} Every decoder answers @code{(Result string _)}, and the
    message names the path:

    @codeblock[#:lang "text"]|{
    Package: the map has no :version entry
    Package.name: expected a symbol
    Package.deps: Dep: the map has no :version entry
    line 1, column 12: Package: this map is written flat, so a '(' here is an entry of the other form
    }|

    and, for a tagged form:

    @codeblock[#:lang "text"]|{
    Package: there is no (version ...) clause
    Package.name: expected a string
    Package.name: a clause holds a field name and one value
    Package: a clause begins with a field name
    line 1, column 8: Package: expected (package ...), and this is (banana ...)
    }|

    The one-pass and the tree decoders give the same message for the same
    document, word for word, which is asserted rather than assumed: the
    codec tests check each refusal through both paths. A @em{structural}
    refusal carries a line and column, because the reader knows one and a
    decoder walking a tree does not; the sentence under it is the same.
    @code{TestFiles/218_bjodat_forms.bjo} asserts both halves: the
    agreement, and the two cases where only the position differs.

    @strong{What to watch out for.}

    @itemlist{
      @item{@strong{An unknown key is read and ignored}, and so is an unknown
      clause, at any arity. It has to be @em{read}: finding where a value
      ends means parsing it. Whether a manifest format should instead refuse
      a misspelt @code{:verison} is still open: refusing is safer, ignoring
      is what makes a file forward-compatible with a newer tool, and it may
      want to be a marker rather than a decision taken once for everyone.}
      @item{@strong{A record holds its fields and nothing else}, so a
      manifest that round-trips through one comes back without whatever
      clauses that version of the tool did not know. A tool that edits a
      manifest in place has to work on the @em{tree}. This is the cost of
      the item above, and the two are the same fact.}
      @item{@strong{A key that is not a keyword can never name a field}, so
      it is ignored too. The tree decoder agrees, for its own reason:
      @code{bjodat-ref} only matches a @code{BjoKey}.}
      @item{@strong{A duplicate key: the first wins}, in both decoders. The
      later one is still read, and then dropped. The same holds for a
      repeated clause.}
      @item{@strong{An @code{(Option (List %a))} holding @code{(Some Nil)}
      writes @code{()} and reads back as @code{None}}, because @code{None}
      is written as the empty list. Nothing else collides.}
      @item{@strong{The one-pass decoder does not track nesting depth across
      records.} Each generated decoder starts a fresh budget, so a chain of
      nested @em{record types} is bounded by the types rather than by
      @code{bjodat-max-depth}. The values inside a field are bounded
      normally.}
    }

    @strong{Speed.} 20 000 records, 2.36 MB, best of 20 on one machine.
    Five scalar fields:

    @read-table{
    | text to records, one pass                                       | 376–383 ns/rec | about 310 MB/s |
    | text to records, via tree                                       | 478–492 ns/rec | about 245 MB/s |
    | the same through @code{(text json)} + @code{(text json-codec)}  | 582–599 ns/rec | about 215 MB/s |
    }

    @strong{A record whose fields are sequences gains nothing.} The same
    corpus with a @code{(Vec string)} and an @code{(Option string)} field
    measures 725–737 ns/rec one-pass against 708–711 through the tree: a
    wash, and on some runs marginally worse.

    That is the design, not a defect. The one-pass decoder saves the
    @em{entry}: the key's string, its interned @code{Keyword}, the
    @code{BjoKey} node, the vec slot it lived in, and the scan over it. It
    does not save the @em{value}: each still becomes a @code{Bjodat} and goes
    through its field type's own instance, which is what keeps a
    hand-written instance working and every message identical. Where the
    values dominate, there is nothing left for it to take away.

    So reach for @code{bjodat-parse-<Name>} by default, and do not expect it
    to be the thing that makes a sequence-heavy document fast. If it
    matters, measure both: they are both there and they agree. Numbers, and
    what did not move, are in @code{bench/BASELINE.md}.
    """))

;; ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
;; The generator
;; ---------------------------------------------------------------------------

(:doc def/bjodat-type
  (summary "Declares a record and the codecs that read it from bjodat and write it back.")
  ;; The form is written with one variable for the whole declaration: a
  ;; published doc is read back as bjodat, where a lone `:` is not a value.
  (form (def/bjodat-type declaration))
  (arg declaration """
    A record's declaration, @code{(: Name (Record field ...))}, or
    @code{(: Name (Record #:tag tag field ...))} for a record written as a
    tagged form. @code{Name} may be @code{(Name %a ...)} for a generic
    record, and then each generated instance wants the same trait of every
    type variable. A field is @code{(: name type)}, then any of the markers
    below.
    """)
  (see ->bjodat bjodat-> parse-one-pass-all)
  (example """
    (def/bjodat-type
      (: Package (Record (: name Symbol)
                         (: version string)
                         (: description string #:key :desc)
                         (: authors (Vec string))
                         (: entry string #:default "src/main.bjo")
                         (: private bool #:default #f)
                         (: note (Option string) #:optional)
                         (: deps (Vec Dep)))))
    """)
  (example """
    (def/bjodat-type
      (: Package (Record #:tag package
                         (: name (List Symbol))
                         (: version Version)
                         (: entry string #:default "src/main.bjo")
                         (: depends (List Dep) #:rest #:default Nil))))
    """)
  (reference """
    Declares the record @em{and} its codecs. Without @code{#:tag}, a record
    is a map whose keys are keywords naming its fields. The keys are keyword
    literals in the generated code, so decoding a field through a tree
    compares interned references and never interns anything at run time.

    @strong{Field markers.}

    @read-table{
    | @code{#:key :other-name} | written and read under @code{:other-name} rather than the field's own |
    | @code{#:optional}        | may be absent; absent is @code{None}                                  |
    | @code{#:default <expr>}  | may be absent; absent is that expression, evaluated at decode         |
    | @code{#:rest}            | takes @em{every} value of its clause; a tagged form only              |
    | @code{#:mutable}         | passed through to the record declaration, and means nothing here      |
    }

    @code{#:optional} is exactly @code{#:default None}, and the field's type
    must be an @code{(Option %a)} to hold it. An optional field that is
    @code{None} is not written at all.

    @strong{There is no null.} A key that may be missing is
    @code{#:optional}, and absence is the whole of it: a data format whose
    values are the language's values has no separate empty. Where an
    @code{(Option %a)} @em{is} written out, @code{None} is the empty list
    @code{()}.

    @strong{A record as a tagged form.} With @code{#:tag}, the record has
    a second spelling, and a manifest is what it is for. The second example
    reads and writes

    @codeblock[#:lang "bjolang"]|{
    (package
      (name (bjolang http))
      (version "1.2.3")
      (depends
        (package (name (bjolang cml)) (version (version-at-least "2.0.1")))
        (package (name (bjo core)))))
    }|

    The head symbol names the type and each clause names a field. It diffs a
    line at a time, it nests without a bracket per level, and it is what a
    person writes. The format did not change to allow this:
    @code{(package (name ...) ...)} was already a @code{BjoList} headed by a
    @code{BjoSym}. What @code{#:tag} changes is the @em{record's encoding},
    which shape its generated codec reads and writes, and nothing about the
    reader. A type has one encoding: a value that read as @code{{...}}
    writes back as @code{{...}}, and one that read as @code{(tag ...)}
    writes back as that.

    @strong{The type picks the shape; the shape never picks the type.} A
    @code{BjoList} headed by a symbol is a tagged record where a tagged
    record is expected, a union case where a union is, and a plain list where
    a @code{(List Symbol)} is, which is why a module name can be written
    @code{(std run)}, the way @code{import} writes it, with no ambiguity
    against @code{(git ...)} in the next clause. It is the rule @code{{...}}
    has always run on: a map is a @code{Package} in one field and a lookup
    table in the next, and the field's declared type says which. Nothing in
    the reader commits, and nothing in the reader could.

    @strong{@code{#:tag} is explicit, with no default.} @code{Package}
    spelled @code{package} would be a guess about capital letters, and which
    tag a type is written under belongs in the file's vocabulary. Two types
    may share a tag: @code{bjo/manifest.bjo} has both @code{Package} and
    @code{Dep} under @code{package}, because a dependency @em{is} a package,
    named and constrained rather than described, and which one a form decodes
    into is the field's to say.

    @strong{@code{#:rest} says which clauses may repeat.} A tagged list says
    nothing about whether a clause may appear with many values, so it is
    answered per field, by the type, which is the only place that knows:
    @code{version} holds one and @code{depends} holds many.

    @codeblock[#:lang "bjolang"]|{
    (depends (package ...) (package ...))   ;; (: depends (List Dep) #:rest)
    (version "1.2.3")                       ;; (: version Version)
    }|

    A field is marked; a clause is not repeated. @code{(depends a) (depends b)}
    does @em{not} accumulate: the first clause wins, as the first entry under
    a key does. Accumulating would have meant one rule for maps and another
    for forms. A @code{#:rest} field reads each of its values through the
    element type's own instance, which keeps the depth budget honest: a
    hostile @code{(package (depends (package (depends ...))))} nested five
    hundred deep is refused by @code{bjodat-max-depth} rather than by the
    stack. An empty @code{#:rest} field with a default is not written.

    @strong{An unknown clause is read and ignored, at any arity.} A map's
    unknown key is one value and done; a clause may hold none, one or five,
    and a reader that insisted on one would make every new clause a breaking
    change for every older tool. So a manifest written for a newer
    @code{bjo} still builds under an older one.

    @strong{What a tagged form costs.} Nothing new. A clause is the
    parenthesised map entry with a bare symbol where the keyword was, so the
    tag and every field name are matched against the reader's own buffer: no
    string is built and nothing is interned for any of them.

    @strong{What it generates.} For @code{(: Package (Record ...))}:

    @read-table{
    | @code{(bjodat-parse-Package text)} | @code{(-> string (Result string Package))}: the one to use |
    | @code{(bjodat-read-Package rd)}    | @code{(-> Reader (Result string Package))}: from a port    |
    | @code{(Package->bjodat p)}         | @code{(-> Package Bjodat)}                                |
    | @code{(bjodat->Package v)}         | @code{(-> Bjodat (Result string Package))}                |
    }

    and the @code{->bjodat} and @code{bjodat->} instances the last two stand
    for. The named pair exists because @code{bjodat->} alone is ambiguous at
    a call site; the signature is what fixes the instance.

    @code{bjodat-read-Package} is what to hand @code{read-one-pass-all} or
    @code{parse-one-pass-all} for a document that is a @code{[...]} of
    records:

    @codeblock[#:lang "bjolang"]|{
    (parse-one-pass-all text bjodat-read-Dep)   ;; -> (Result string (List Dep))
    }|

    Pass it @strong{by name}, not wrapped in a @code{(fun (rd) ...)}. A
    lambda body is a member of its own and is sealed to the sync copy of what
    it calls, so the wrapped form makes a bjoroutine park its thread. The
    blocking lint says so.
    """))

;; ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
;; The traits
;; ---------------------------------------------------------------------------

(:doc ->bjodat
  (summary "Writing a value as bjodat: a type with an instance can be a field.")
  (tparam %a "The type written.")
  (arg v "The value.")
  (returns "It, as a @code{Bjodat}.")
  (see bjodat-> def/bjodat-type)
  (reference """
    @code{def/bjodat-type} writes an instance for each record it declares.
    Instances ship for @code{long}, @code{int}, @code{double}, @code{bool},
    @code{string}, @code{char}, @code{Symbol}, @code{Keyword} and
    @code{Bjodat} itself, and for @code{(Option %a)}, @code{(Vec %a)} and
    @code{(List %a)} when the element type has one. @code{None} is written
    as the empty list @code{()}; a @code{Vec} is a @code{[...]} and a
    @code{List} a @code{(...)}.
    """))

(:doc bjodat->
  (summary "Reading a value from bjodat: a type with an instance can be a field.")
  (tparam %a "The type read.")
  (arg v "The value read.")
  (returns "@code{(Ok x)}, or @code{(Err message)} when the value is not of the type's shape.")
  (see ->bjodat def/bjodat-type)
  (reference """
    Instances ship for the same types as @code{->bjodat}'s. Each takes only
    its own shape: a symbol and a keyword are different values, and a field
    asking for one does not take the other; a @code{Vec} wants a
    @code{[...]} and a @code{List} a @code{(...)}. The one widening is
    @code{double}, which takes an integer too. An @code{(Option %a)} reads
    the empty list as @code{None} and anything else as @code{Some}. A
    refusal is a message such as @code{expected a string}, which the
    generated decoders prefix with the path to the field.
    """))

;; ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
;; What generated field code calls. Not an API for people.
;; ---------------------------------------------------------------------------

(:doc bjodat-map
  (summary "A value's map entries, or a refusal. Generated code calls it; not an API for people.")
  (arg owner "The record's name, for the message.")
  (arg v "The value.")
  (returns "The entries of a @code{BjoMap}, or @code{owner: expected a map}.")
  (see bjodat-field))

(:doc bjodat-field
  (summary "A required field, from a map's entries. Generated code calls it; not an API for people.")
  (arg owner "The record's name, for messages.")
  (arg entries "The map's entries.")
  (arg key "The field's key.")
  (returns "The first value under the key, converted by its instance; a refusal names the record and the key.")
  (see bjodat-field/default bjodat-need-field))

(:doc bjodat-field/default
  (summary "A field with a fallback, from a map's entries. Generated code calls it; not an API for people.")
  (arg owner "The record's name, for messages.")
  (arg entries "The map's entries.")
  (arg key "The field's key.")
  (arg fallback "What an absent field is.")
  (returns "The first value under the key, converted by its instance, or the fallback when there is none.")
  (see bjodat-field))

(:doc bjodat-read-field
  (summary "Reads a field's value off the reader and converts it. Generated code calls it; not an API for people.")
  (arg owner "The record's name, for messages.")
  (arg key "The field's key, for messages.")
  (arg rd "The reader, standing at the value.")
  (returns "The value, converted by its instance; a refusal names the record and the key.")
  (see bjodat-need-field))

(:doc bjodat-need-field
  (summary "A required field the one-pass decoder may not have met. Generated code calls it; not an API for people.")
  (arg slot "What the decoder found, if anything.")
  (arg owner "The record's name, for the message.")
  (arg key "The field's key, for the message.")
  (returns "The value, or @code{bjodat-field}'s refusal word for word, so a report does not reveal which decoder read the document.")
  (see bjodat-field))

(:doc bjodat-key
  (summary "A keyword as a BjoKey, for a generated encoder. Not an API for people.")
  (arg k "The keyword.")
  (returns "@code{(BjoKey k)}.")
  (see bjodat-entries))

(:doc bjodat-entries
  (summary "A map from entries pushed in reverse, for a generated encoder. Not an API for people.")
  (arg acc "Value, key, value, key, ..., last entry first.")
  (returns "The @code{BjoMap}, entries in order.")
  (see bjodat-key))

;; The same, for a record written as a tagged form.

(:doc bjodat-clauses
  (summary "A tagged form's clauses, with its tag checked. Generated code calls it; not an API for people.")
  (arg owner "The record's name, for messages.")
  (arg tag "The head the form must have.")
  (arg v "The value.")
  (returns "The clauses, or a refusal when the head is not the tag or a clause does not begin with a field name.")
  (see bjodat-clause-field))

(:doc bjodat-clause-field
  (summary "A required field, from a tagged form's clauses. Generated code calls it; not an API for people.")
  (arg owner "The record's name, for messages.")
  (arg cs "The clauses.")
  (arg name "The field's name.")
  (returns "The one value of the first clause under the name, converted; a refusal when there is none or it holds more than one.")
  (see bjodat-clause-field/default bjodat-clause-rest))

(:doc bjodat-clause-field/default
  (summary "A field with a fallback, from a tagged form's clauses. Generated code calls it; not an API for people.")
  (arg owner "The record's name, for messages.")
  (arg cs "The clauses.")
  (arg name "The field's name.")
  (arg fallback "What an absent field is.")
  (returns "The one value of the first clause under the name, converted, or the fallback when there is none.")
  (see bjodat-clause-field))

(:doc bjodat-clause-rest
  (summary "A required #:rest field, from a tagged form's clauses. Generated code calls it; not an API for people.")
  (arg owner "The record's name, for messages.")
  (arg cs "The clauses.")
  (arg name "The field's name.")
  (returns "Every value of the first clause under the name, each converted; a refusal when there is none.")
  (see bjodat-clause-rest/default bjodat-clause-field))

(:doc bjodat-clause-rest/default
  (summary "A #:rest field with a fallback, from a tagged form's clauses. Generated code calls it; not an API for people.")
  (arg owner "The record's name, for messages.")
  (arg cs "The clauses.")
  (arg name "The field's name.")
  (arg fallback "What an absent field is.")
  (returns "Every value of the first clause under the name, each converted, or the fallback when there is none.")
  (see bjodat-clause-rest))

(:doc bjodat-read-clause
  (summary "Reads a clause's one value off the reader, and its ). Generated code calls it; not an API for people.")
  (arg owner "The record's name, for messages.")
  (arg name "The field's name, for messages.")
  (arg rd "The reader, standing at the value.")
  (returns "The value, converted; a refusal names the record and the field.")
  (see bjodat-read-rest bjodat-need-clause))

(:doc bjodat-read-rest
  (summary "Reads every value of a clause off the reader, and its ). Generated code calls it; not an API for people.")
  (arg owner "The record's name, for messages.")
  (arg name "The field's name, for messages.")
  (arg rd "The reader, standing at the first value.")
  (returns "The values, each converted; a refusal names the record and the field.")
  (see bjodat-read-clause))

(:doc bjodat-need-clause
  (summary "A required clause the one-pass decoder may not have met. Generated code calls it; not an API for people.")
  (arg slot "What the decoder found, if anything.")
  (arg owner "The record's name, for the message.")
  (arg name "The field's name, for the message.")
  (returns "The value, or @code{bjodat-clause-field}'s refusal word for word.")
  (see bjodat-clause-field))

(:doc bjodat-clause
  (summary "A clause holding one value, for a generated encoder. Not an API for people.")
  (arg name "The field's name.")
  (arg v "The value.")
  (returns "@code{(name v)}.")
  (see bjodat-clause-many bjodat-form-value))

(:doc bjodat-clause-many
  (summary "A clause holding many values, for a generated encoder. Not an API for people.")
  (arg name "The field's name.")
  (arg vs "The values.")
  (returns "@code{(name v ...)}.")
  (see bjodat-clause bjodat-clause-values))

(:doc bjodat-clause-values
  (summary "Each element written as bjodat, for a #:rest field. Not an API for people.")
  (arg xs "The field's elements.")
  (returns "Each converted by its instance.")
  (see bjodat-clause-many))

(:doc bjodat-form-value
  (summary "A tagged form from clauses pushed in reverse, for a generated encoder. Not an API for people.")
  (arg tag "The head.")
  (arg acc "The clauses, last first.")
  (returns "@code{(tag clause ...)}, clauses in order.")
  (see bjodat-clause))
